    <text x="900" y="320" font-family="PM" font-size="9.5" fill="#6f6d68" letter-spacing="1">FROZEN · THE RADAR NEVER PULLS THESE BACK</text>
   </svg>
   <div class="legend"><span><i></i>Active</span><span><i style="border-style:dashed"></i>Parked, needs data</span><span><i style="border-color:#ff5a1f"></i>Waiting for a human</span><span><i style="background:#f6f2ea"></i>Frozen</span></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 06 WHO DOES WHAT -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">06</span> · Where AI fits</span><span>Executor, not the brain</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">The AI fills forms. The system makes the rules.</h2><p class="rv d1">Job Beast prepares everything and owns every decision. A browser operator, like Claude in Chrome or a Playwright script, only executes the live form, and <b>it isn't allowed to invent an answer</b>.</p></div>
  <div class="split2 rv">
   <div><h5>JOB BEAST OWNS</h5><div class="big">State, evidence, ranking and rules</div><ul><li>Discovery, dedup and ranking</li><li>Eligibility gates and the evidence bank</li><li>The tailored résumé and application packet</li><li>Importing results and verifying outcomes</li></ul></div>
   <div><h5>THE OPERATOR DOES</h5><div class="big">Fills the live application</div><ul><li>Reads the packet and operator instructions</li><li>Fills fields with prepared, confirmed answers</li><li>Submits only when I authorize it</li><li>Writes back a result file</li></ul></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bound rv">
   <div><b>CAPTCHA &amp; MFA</b><p>Never automated. The job is parked for me.</p></div>
   <div><b>Legal questions</b><p>Answered only from answers I confirmed before.</p></div>
   <div><b>Untrusted text</b><p>Instructions inside a job posting are never followed.</p></div>
   <div><b>Private data</b><p>Voluntary EEO answers never enter a packet.</p></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 07 PROOF -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">07</span> · Proof</span><span>Real-world iteration</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Run against the real world, 100+ times.</h2><p class="rv d1">Stale listings, JavaScript-only pages, reworded legal questions, custom dropdowns, verification codes and confirmation pages that never arrive. <b>Every ugly edge case improved the architecture.</b></p></div>
  <div class="proof rv" style="border-top:1px solid var(--ink)"><div><b>100<em>+</em></b><small>real internship application workflows during development</small></div><div><b>9</b><small>core application states in a persisted state machine</small></div><div><b>0</b><small>invented skills, metrics or legal answers, by design</small></div><div><b>CI</b><small>offline test suite plus secret scanning on every push</small></div></div>
  <div class="note rv"><div class="m">Tested on</div><div><div class="atss"><span>Greenhouse</span><span>Lever</span><span>Ashby</span><span>SmartRecruiters</span><span>Workday</span><span>Jobvite</span><span>+ more</span></div>
   <p style="margin-top:18px"><b>“An action is not the same as an outcome.”</b> Clicking Submit proves nothing: forms reject silently and sessions expire. A job only becomes SUBMITTED with an employer confirmation. Otherwise it's SUBMITTED_UNVERIFIED, which still blocks a duplicate application.</p></div></div>
 </section>

 <!-- LEARNED -->
 <section class="learn rv">
  <div><div class="m">What I learned</div><div class="m" style="color:var(--or);margin-top:10px">In my words</div></div>
  <div>
   <blockquote>Building something that works once is very different from building something <span>you can trust repeatedly.</span></blockquote>
   <p>Most of the interesting work ended up being around edge cases, state transitions, verification and deciding when the system should stop and ask for help. That changed how I think about software. Now, when I build something, I automatically start thinking about how it fails, not just how it works.</p>
   <div class="sig">— Vishalkiran</div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <section class="sec" style="margin-top:110px">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">08</span> · What's next</span><span>From the roadmap</span></div>
